# 🔧 Troubleshooting & Debugging Guide – AWS 3-Tier Architecture (Real-World Issues & Solutions)

During the implementation of this AWS 3-Tier Architecture project, I encountered several real-world issues. Instead of recreating the infrastructure, I followed a systematic troubleshooting approach to identify the root cause and resolve each problem. These experiences significantly improved my debugging skills and strengthened my understanding of AWS networking, security, and application connectivity.

---

## 1. Secrets Manager Authentication Issue

### Problem

The application failed to connect to the Amazon Aurora database due to a database authentication error.

### Root Cause

The credentials stored in AWS Secrets Manager did not match the actual database username and password.

### Troubleshooting Steps

* Verified the database endpoint and port.
* Reviewed the secret stored in AWS Secrets Manager.
* Compared the stored username and password with the Amazon Aurora database credentials.
* Updated the incorrect secret values.
* Restarted the application and validated the database connection.

### Resolution

After updating the credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, the application successfully connected to the database.

### Key Learning

Never hardcode credentials. Always verify Secrets Manager values when troubleshooting database authentication issues.

---

## 2. Private EC2 Internet Connectivity Issue

### Problem

The private EC2 instance could not access the internet, and `ping 8.8.8.8` was unsuccessful.

### Root Cause Analysis

I systematically verified each networking component instead of making assumptions.

### Troubleshooting Steps

* Verified the NAT Gateway was deployed correctly.
* Confirmed the private subnet route table pointed to the NAT Gateway.
* Checked the Internet Gateway attachment to the VPC.
* Reviewed Security Group outbound rules.
* Validated Network ACL rules.
* Confirmed the EC2 IAM Role and Session Manager configuration.
* Tested internet connectivity after each configuration change.

### Resolution

After correcting the networking configuration, the private EC2 instance successfully accessed the internet.

### Key Learning

When debugging networking issues, validate each AWS networking component one by one instead of changing multiple settings simultaneously.

---

# Lessons Learned

* Follow a structured troubleshooting approach.
* Identify the root cause before applying fixes.
* Validate every networking component independently.
* Use AWS managed services such as IAM Roles and Secrets Manager instead of hardcoded credentials.
* Real-world cloud engineering involves debugging production issues as much as deploying infrastructure.

These troubleshooting scenarios enhanced my practical understanding of AWS networking, security, application connectivity, and production-ready infrastructure deployment.
